  "name": "human_arm_like_v1",
  "description": "Design-study 6R arm with human-limb proportions: upper arm 0.31 m, forearm 0.26 m, hand 0.12 m from the wrist joint to the fingertip. Shoulder height 0.90 m above the hip base; the kinematic base is mounted at the LEFT SHOULDER (base_origin = [0, +0.19, 0]) while the trunk and the stereo head stay centred at y = 0 — like a human, the head is in the middle and the arm hangs off to the side. Masses are scaled from adult-male arm segments (total ~5.0 kg). q2 = q3 = 0 is the neutral hanging pose (arm straight down alongside the trunk); raising the arm forward-horizontal is +90 deg. Joint DOFs are anatomical: j1 trunk/shoulder-girdle rotation (vertical axis), j2 shoulder flexion/extension (mediolateral axis), j3 elbow flexion (mediolateral axis, hyperextension forbidden), j4 wrist flexion/extension (mediolateral axis at the wrist), j5 wrist radial/ulnar deviation (anterior-posterior axis), j6 hand axial rotation (forearm axis). IMPORTANT DH-anchor limitation: with the vertical-trunk/horizontal-shoulder anchor, true forearm pronation/supination CANNOT be represented as a rotation axis (a DH twist rotates about the x-axis of the preceding frame, which is horizontal when the arm hangs vertically, so it can never align with the near-vertical forearm). The three wrist DOFs (j4/j5/j6) model flexion/extension, radial/ulnar deviation and axial hand rotation; forearm pronation/supination is NOT represented (the palm cannot be rotated from palm-forward to palm-backward). Glenohumeral abduction/adduction and isolated internal/external rotation are also not modeled; j1 (trunk/scapular-girdle rotation) plus j2 partially cover the transverse reach component. Parameters are representative, not measured.",
